24.2  ~/.ssh/config, jump hosts and host keys

TL;DR
  - ~/.ssh/config gives each server a short name with its address, user, port
    and key: ssh db instead of a long command line.
  - ProxyJump reaches a server through another one (a bastion), end to end
    encrypted, with your keys staying on your machine.
  - The first connection asks you to trust the server's host key and saves it in
    known_hosts; later, a different key stops the connection.

      Host db
          HostName 10.0.4.12
          User alice
          ProxyJump bastion.example.com

On your machine
  Keys      id_ed25519 (ssh-ed25519), id_rsa_old (ssh-rsa) open to others (644):
            ssh refuses it
  Config    3 host entries in ~/.ssh/config, with jump hosts
  Known     4 host keys trusted in known_hosts, names hashed
  Login     1 key may log in here as you (authorized_keys)
  Agent     none in this session: keys with a passphrase ask for it every time
  Server    port 2222 · password login no · root login prohibit-password
            (default)

Pitfalls
  - ssh keeps the first value it finds for each option: put specific Host blocks
    first and Host * last.
  - "REMOTE HOST IDENTIFICATION HAS CHANGED": the server was reinstalled, or
    someone is in between. Check with its admin, then remove the old key:
    ssh-keygen -R host.
  - Changing the server's port or disabling passwords in sshd_config: keep a
    working session open while you test a second one.
  - ssh -G host prints the options that apply after reading every config file:
    the quickest way to see why a setting is ignored.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 24.2 --why

Related
  Commands  ssh, ssh-keygen
  Paths     ~/.ssh/config, ~/.ssh/known_hosts, /etc/ssh/sshd_config
  See also  idk 24.1 SSH keys and the agent
  See also  idk 24.3 SSH tunnels and port forwarding
